.TH ENCOPT 5 "2026-11-10" "encopt 1.1" "pdp64 file formats"
.SH NAME
encopt \- input, weights and output files of encopt(1)
.SH DESCRIPTION
Both files are plain text, one record per line, fields separated by white space.  \fB#\fR starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.  Names and mnemonics
contain no white space.
.SH INPUT FILE
The first record is \fBencopt-input 2\fR (or \fBencopt-input 1\fR, the older form of the \fBdemand\fR line, below).  The others, in any order but each \fBdemand\fR after the \fBform\fR lines it names:
.TP
.BI "budget " B " [" N ]
Code space the forms may use, in first-parcel patterns of \fIN\fR (default 65536 = 2^16).  The forced forms count against it, the candidates get what they leave.
\fBexport.py\fR writes 65536: no reserve, the free-space policy is zero.  The study kept 5% free: 62259.  Nothing but the budget keeps a pattern out: a pattern that no leaf takes
is an illegal instruction, and the unassigned sub-opcodes of a leaf are illegal inside the leaf.
.TP
.BI lam " x"
Informational: the bytes charged for the extra instruction of a compare followed by a branch where no compare-and-branch form reaches (already part of the \fBbase\fR of the demands).
.TP
.BI "form " name " " role " " parcels " " L " " xbits " " mnemonic ...
A form.
.I role
is \fBforced\fR (always present) or \fBcand\fR (a candidate; candidates are numbered 0, 1, ... in the order of the file).
.I parcels
is the length of the instruction in 16-bit parcels, \fIL\fR the prefix length of one mnemonic of the form (16*parcels minus the operand bits minus
the bits of an extended sub-opcode), \fIxbits\fR the bits of the extended sub-opcode (0: none).  Without an extended sub-opcode the mnemonic list is split into
binary-aligned groups (the largest power of two first), each its own leaf with the prefix length L minus the group's exponent, and the form takes
(number of mnemonics) * 2^(16\-L) patterns; with one the whole list is a single leaf of prefix length L taking 2^(16\-L) patterns.
.TP
.BI "demand " static " " dynamic " " base " " ops " " k " " c1 " ... " ck
One class of instructions of the corpus (format 2).
.I static
is how often it occurs in the corpus and
.I dynamic
how often a profile executes it (0 without a profile): the two weights of the objectives, see \fBencopt\fR(1).
.I base
is the bytes the forced forms give it (99 for no form at all: the credit of the byte model; the price of an unreachable compare-and-branch, cmp + bCC + lam, when a fallback exists),
.I ops
the operations it takes under the forced forms (1; 2 for a compare-and-branch that no form reaches; 99 for no form at all: used by the objective \fBexec\fR), and
.IR c1 " ... " ck
the candidates (their numbers) that cover it.  A candidate that is not cheaper than \fIbase\fR is ignored for the demand (in \fBexec\fR the cost of a candidate is k plus its bytes,
that of the base k times \fIops\fR plus \fIbase\fR).  Demands with the same \fIbase\fR, \fIops\fR and candidates are added up into one line (export.py does that: 50,000 distinct
instructions become about 830 lines).
.TP
.BI "demand " weight " " base " " k " " c1 " ... " ck
Format 1: one weight (static count or execution count: the objective is the sum of weight times bytes), no \fIops\fR; read as static = dynamic = \fIweight\fR and
\fIops\fR 1 (99 when \fIbase\fR is 99).
.TP
.BI committed " name"
A candidate that the grammar in force contains, in the order of its catalogue (the order fixes the prefixes).  Used by \fB\-C\fR and for the comparison in the result.
.TP
.BI require " name" "\fR, \fPforbid " name
Constraints on the choice, as the options \fB\-r\fR and \fB\-x\fR.
.SH WEIGHTS FILE
A file of the counts of single instructions, read by \fBexport.py \-\-weights\fR (and written by \fBprofile.py\fR); not read by \fBencopt\fR itself.  The first record is \fBencopt-weights 1\fR;
a line that starts with \fB#\fR is a comment (an immediate operand has a # inside the line); the others:
.TP
.BI "weight " kind " " count " " mnemonic " " operands
.I kind
is \fBdynamic\fR (how often the instruction was executed: becomes the dynamic weight of its demand class) or \fBstatic\fR (how often it occurs: added to the static weight; the corpus
itself needs no such lines).  An instruction that is not in the corpus gets the static weight 0 and takes part in the model all the same.
The instruction is a distinct instruction of the corpus: the mnemonic and the operands in assembly notation, separated by commas without a space between them, with the values that decide whether a form
fits: \fBr5\fR, \fBsp\fR, \fBfp\fR; \fB#\fIN\fR an immediate; \fB@\fIN\fR an absolute address; \fIN\fB(r\fIb\fB)\fR with a base register (\fB(dp)\fR for the DP-relative form);
\fIN\fB(r\fIb\fB)(r\fIx\fB*\fIs\fB)\fR indexed; \fB(r\fIb\fB)+\fIN\fR and \fB(r\fIb\fB)-\fIN\fR with a stride; \fBpc+\fIN\fR or \fBpc\-\fIN\fR a pc-relative operand (the bytes from the first parcel of the
instruction to its target).  A condition or a system register of \fBset\fR, \fBmfsr\fR and \fBmtsr\fR is written as its number, \fB#2\fR for \fBset eq,r0\fR (the numbers of
\fIspec/pdp64-mnemonics.hbnf\fR).  The same instruction on several lines is added up.  Counts are those of whole programs, so a pc-relative distance or an absolute address
is the linker's; \fBencdis \-k\fR (tools/encdis.c) writes this notation from a \fBsim64 \-P\fR profile.
.SH OUTPUT FILE
The first record is \fBencopt-output 2\fR (format 1, written by encopt 1.1, had no \fBobjective\fR, \fBoperation-cost\fR and \fBmetrics\fR records).  Then:
.TP
.BI objective " name"
\fBstatic\fR, \fBfetch\fR or \fBexec\fR.
.TP
.BI operation-cost " k"
\fBexec\fR only: the bytes that one operation counts for.
.TP
.BI status " s"
\fBoptimal\fR: the search finished; the selection is optimal for the model of the input (the candidates offered, the budget, the demands).
\fBoptimal-within-constraint-set\fR: as that, with \fBrequire\fR/\fBforbid\fR constraints in force.
\fBbest-found\fR: a limit stopped the search; \fBlower-bound-credit\fR and \fBgap-credit\fR say how far from proved.
\fBcommitted-selection\fR: written by \fB\-C\fR.  \fBinfeasible\fR: the constraints cannot be met.
.TP
.BI objective-credit " v"
The value minimised, in the objective in force: the sum of weight times cost (bytes; for exec k times operations plus bytes), a demand that no chosen form shortens counting its base (99 for none).
.TP
.B lower-bound-credit, gap-credit
Proved lower bound and the difference (0 when optimal).
.TP
.BI objective-bytes " v" ", unencodable-weight " w
The weighted bytes (weights of the objective in force) without the demands that nothing encodes, and the weight of those.
.TP
.BI patterns " used budget"
First-parcel patterns taken by forced and chosen forms, and the budget.
.TP
.BI compare " name " credit " c " bytes " b " unencodable " u " patterns " p " vs-committed " d
For \fBcommitted\fR (the selection of the input), \fBgreedy\fR (the study's greedy rule: whole candidates by bytes saved per pattern^alpha, five exponents),
\fBgreedy-refined\fR (that with a local search) and \fBencopt\fR; \fId\fR is the change of the byte objective against the committed selection.
.TP
.BI metrics " name " static-bytes " s " fetch-bytes " f " operations " o " unencodable-static " us " unencodable-dynamic " ud
After each \fBcompare\fR record, the three measures of that selection whatever the objective: the bytes of the corpus (static counts times size), the bytes fetched (dynamic counts times size),
the operations executed (dynamic counts; a compare-and-branch that no chosen or forced form reaches is two), and the static and dynamic weight of the instructions that nothing encodes (left out of the three).
.TP
.BI chosen " name"
A chosen candidate, in the order of the file.
.TP
.BI kraft " used total"
Patterns taken by the assignment of all leaves.
.TP
.BI leaf " form k L prefix n mnemonic ..."
A leaf: the form, the number of the leaf within the form (0 when the form has an extended sub-opcode), the prefix length, the prefix as binary digits, the number of
mnemonics (a power of two unless extended) and the mnemonics in the order of the sub-opcode.  Leaves are listed shortest prefix first, in the order of the assignment
(forced forms in the order of the file, then the chosen candidates in the order of the file).  The assignment is canonical: shorter prefixes first, each placed at
the next multiple of its size; it is the one the study's model (enc.py, Code._assign) computes, so a leaf list in the order of a grammar's catalogue reproduces its prefixes.
.PP
The output holds no times and no counters, so equal inputs give equal files.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR encopt (1)
